<% content_for :title, "Theming — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "Shadwire's semantic tokens: where they live, what each one controls, how to change your brand colour, and how to keep your edits across updates." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Theming",
        description: "Every colour comes from a token. Retheming means editing two lists of CSS variables; no component has to change." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Where the tokens live") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      In <%= docs_code("vendor/shadwire/shadwire.css") %>, installed by
      <%= docs_code("shadwire init") %> and imported from the application's Tailwind entrypoint.
      <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire status --json") %> reports the real path under
      <%= docs_code("tailwind.css") %>.
    </p>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">The file has three layers:</p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layers], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("@theme inline") %> is what turns <%= docs_code("--primary") %> into the
      <%= docs_code("bg-primary") %> and <%= docs_code("text-primary") %> utilities. The colours
      are OKLCH.
    </p>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "Edit :root and .dark, never @theme inline" } do %>
      The <code>@theme inline</code> block is plumbing: it maps a variable to a utility. The
      theme itself lives in the <code>:root</code> (light) and <code>.dark</code> blocks.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("The tokens") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Every surface has a matching <%= docs_code("-foreground") %>. Always use them as a pair:
      <%= docs_code("bg-muted text-muted-foreground") %>, never
      <%= docs_code("bg-muted") %> with the default text colour.
    </p>

    <%= render "docs/table",
          headers: [ "Token", "Controls", "Used by" ],
          rows: @tokens.map { |token| [ docs_code(token[:token]), token[:controls], token[:used_by] ] } %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Changing the brand colour") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Set <%= docs_code("--primary") %> and <%= docs_code("--primary-foreground") %> in both
      blocks. Every component that uses <%= docs_code("bg-primary") %> follows.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:brand], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Pick the foreground by its contrast against that surface, and check it in both modes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Adding a token") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Declare the variable in both blocks and map it in <%= docs_code("@theme inline") %> so
      Tailwind generates the utilities:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:new_token], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      From then on <%= docs_code("bg-success") %> and
      <%= docs_code("text-success-foreground") %> behave like any other token.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Corner radius") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("--radius") %> feeds the derived
      <%= docs_code("radius-sm") %> / <%= docs_code("radius-md") %> /
      <%= docs_code("radius-lg") %> scale, so changing it reshapes cards, inputs, buttons and
      popovers at once.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:radius], language: :css) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Adding a variant") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Variants are frozen Ruby hashes inside the component's class. Since the file is yours,
      adding one is adding a key:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:variant], language: :ruby) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:variant_usage]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      That is a local edit: <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire diff") %> will report the file as
      <%= docs_code("modified") %>, and <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire update") %> would overwrite it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Keeping your edits") %>

    <ol class="ml-4 list-decimal space-y-2 leading-7 text-muted-foreground marker:font-medium marker:text-foreground">
      <li><%= docs_code("bin/shadwire diff <name>") %>: see what you changed.</li>
      <li>
        <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire update <name>") %> <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">without</strong>
        <%= docs_code("--yes") %>: it asks about each modified file and shows the diff.
      </li>
      <li>Reapply your edits on top.</li>
    </ol>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:keep_edits], language: :shell) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Prefer adding (a new variant key, a new token) over rewriting existing classes. Additive
      changes survive updates with far less conflict.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
